program AgentsExe;

// WHICH BLOCK OF THE WORLD -- one square of `BlockGrid.SIDE` cells, the unit everything spatial is
// bucketed by: who stands near whom, which meshes get drawn, which patch of ground gets rebaked.
//
// IT EXISTS BECAUSE `BlockChain` HAS THREE KINDS OF NUMBER AND HAD ONE TYPE. In nine methods it
// takes and answers a block, a slot and a cell, all of them `int`:
//
//     firstIn(block) -> slot        blockOf(slot) -> block        cellOf(slot) -> cell
//
// The first two are exact inverses with identical signatures. Swapping them compiles, and what
// comes back is a real block or a real person somewhere else in the world -- the failure this whole
// family of types is here to stop, and the one that never crashes.
//
// THE SLOT STAYS BARE, and that is decided rather than missed: one `BlockChain` files the people and
// another files the animals, so "a slot" is the only honest thing the class can say about what it
// holds. Its callers put the name back on at the line where they know whose it is.
public bundle Agents {
    public namespace World {

        public newtype BlockId = int;
    }
}
